<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Methodism</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">18th century–</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Methodist Church, Wesleyan Methodism</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Methodism is a Protestant movement that began in the 18th-century evangelical revival led by John and Charles Wesley within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-england">Church of England</a>, and became separate churches after Wesley's death. Its stress on personal conversion, preaching and organised local societies spread it widely in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a> and the colonies. Wesleyan missionaries were active in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> from the early 19th century.</p></div>
